A robust implementation would make the square a real button, so it is keyboard-accessible and clicking it toggles exactly once. For example:

```html
<script>
  class XDetails extends HTMLElement {
    constructor() {
      super();
      this.attachShadow({ mode: "open" });
      this.expanded = this.hasAttribute("expanded");

      this.shadowRoot.innerHTML = `
        <style>
          :host { display: block; }
          .header {
            display: flex;
            align-items: center;
            gap: .6rem;
            border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc;
            padding-bottom: .4rem;
            font-size: 1.2rem;
          }
          .indicator {
            width: 1.5rem;
            height: 1.5rem;
            padding: 0;
            border: 1px solid currentColor;
            background: transparent;
            cursor: pointer;
          }
          #details[hidden], #intro[hidden] { display: none; }
        </style>

        <div class="header">
          <button class="indicator" type="button" aria-expanded="false"></button>
          <slot name="header"></slot>
        </div>
        <div id="intro"><slot name="intro"></slot></div>
        <div id="details" hidden><slot name="details"></slot></div>
        <button class="more" type="button">expand more</button>
      `;

      this.indicator = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".indicator");
      this.details = this.shadowRoot.querySelector("#details");
      this.more = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".more");

      this.indicator.addEventListener("click", () => this.toggle());
      this.more.addEventListener("click", () => this.toggle());
      this.update();
    }

    toggle() {
      this.expanded = !this.expanded;
      this.update();
    }

    update() {
      this.indicator.textContent = this.expanded ? "−" : "+";
      this.indicator.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(this.expanded));
      this.details.hidden = !this.expanded;
      this.more.textContent = this.expanded ? "show less" : "expand more";
    }
  }

  customElements.define("x-details", XDetails);
</script>
```

Both answers provide a relevant Shadow DOM component with the requested slots, visible intro, and expandable details. However, **both have a double-toggle bug**: the indicator has its own click handler, and its click also bubbles to the header’s click handler, undoing the toggle. In the example above, the indicator is a button with its own handler and is not handled again by a clickable header.

Assistant A styles the indicator as a square and changes the lower button’s text to “collapse” when expanded, so it matches the requested appearance and behavior more closely. Its indicator is a non-semantic `div`, though, and its double-toggle bug means clicking that square does not actually change the state.

Assistant B also has the double-toggle bug. Its indicator has no border, so it does not meet the square requirement, and its “Expand more” button remains visible with the same label when expanded. Its `disconnectedCallback` also fails to remove listeners because it creates new bound functions instead of removing the original handler references. B does provide an optional initially-expanded state, but that does not outweigh the issues above.

Overall, A is somewhat closer to the requested component, though both need correction.

My final verdict is: [[A>B]]

