        <div class="details" hidden><slot name="details"></slot></div>
        <button class="more" type="button">expand more</button>
      `;

      this.onToggle = this.toggle.bind(this);
    }

    connectedCallback() {
      this.headerButton = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".header");
      this.indicator = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".indicator");
      this.details = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".details");
      this.moreButton = this.shadowRoot.querySelector(".more");

      this.headerButton.addEventListener("click", this.onToggle);
      this.moreButton.addEventListener("click", this.onToggle);
      this.syncState();
    }

    disconnectedCallback() {
      this.headerButton?.removeEventListener("click", this.onToggle);
      this.moreButton?.removeEventListener("click", this.onToggle);
    }

    attributeChangedCallback() {
      this.syncState();
    }

    toggle() {
      this.toggleAttribute("expanded");
    }

    syncState() {
      if (!this.headerButton) return;
      const expanded = this.hasAttribute("expanded");

      this.headerButton.setAttribute("aria-expanded", String(expanded));
      this.indicator.textContent = expanded ? "−" : "+";
      this.details.hidden = !expanded;
      this.moreButton.hidden = expanded;
    }
  }

  customElements.define("x-details", XDetails);
</script>
```

The header toggles the details, the intro stays visible, and the “expand more” button appears only while collapsed. Add the `expanded` attribute to start open.

## Evaluation

Both answers generally implement the requested slotted Shadow DOM component, keep the intro visible, and show the details when expanded.

**Assistant A** has working basic toggle behavior and supports starting with the `expanded` attribute. However, its indicator is not styled as a bordered square, so it misses a specific visual requirement. It also leaves the “Expand more” button visible with that same label when expanded. Its event-listener cleanup is ineffective: `bind(this)` creates a new function each time, so `removeEventListener` does not remove the listeners that were added.

**Assistant B** better matches the requested square indicator and changes the button text to “collapse” when expanded. But it registers click listeners on both the indicator and its containing header. Clicking the indicator therefore toggles twice because the click bubbles to the header, leaving the component unchanged. It also adds anonymous listeners on connection without removing them, so reconnecting the element can accumulate listeners.

B is slightly better overall because its styling more directly meets the square-indicator requirement and it provides an expanded-state button label. Its indicator-click bug is significant, though, and prevents it from being a fully correct implementation.

My final verdict: [[B>A]]

